> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Windows 10 1607+ / 11 x64 only.** Uses Win32 GUI, Job Object, WinEventHook, low-level input hooks, per-monitor DPI awareness v2, and a local Microsoft Edge install.
>
> You do **not** need to install anything into Edge first: this program opens a standalone window with `msedge.exe --app=<tokenUrl>`.

---

## What it does

From dsh 0.1.2 the web root is authenticated: each process prints a one-shot URL with `?token=`. The browser exchanges that token for a session cookie, then lands on a clean root. Opening `http://127.0.0.1:3080/` with no token returns 401.

This repo folds that flow into a console-less exe:

1. Single-instance check: if an instance is already running, just bring its window to the front and exit.
2. Show a native splash immediately (cancellable).
3. Launch `dsh web --no-open` through pnpm's `node.exe`, stdout on a pipe.
4. Parse `dsh web: http://127.0.0.1:<port>/?token=...`.
5. Open Edge `--app=<tokenUrl>` once, maximize it, then publish a tray icon.
6. Closing the window (X / Alt+F4 / Ctrl+Shift+W) only **hides it to the tray**; the backend keeps running.
7. Tray menu → Quit → Job Object kills the node tree and frees the port.
8. Tray menu → Restart → the backend comes back up in place (window and tray stay put); dshmarket's own Restart works too.

---

## Close and restart

| Action | Result |
| :--- | :--- |
| Left-click the title-bar X | Hide to tray, backend keeps running |
| `Alt+F4` (app window focused) | Same |
| `Ctrl+Shift+W` (app window focused) | Same |
| Left-click the tray icon / run the exe again | Bring the existing window to the front |
| Right-click tray icon → Show window | Same |
| **Right-click tray icon → Restart backend** | **Reclaim and relaunch the backend, and swap in a fresh app window (a full refresh, new page)** |
| **Run the exe again with `--restart`** | **Same as the tray item (handy for a shortcut)** |
| Restart from dshmarket | Backend restarts **and the launcher swaps in a fresh window** using the replacement host's new token, which it reads from dshmarket's own restart log — the page is always authenticated |
| Right-click tray icon → Quit and stop backend | Close window + kill process tree + remove tray icon + exit |
| Window destroyed by anything else (taskbar preview close, browser crash, …) | Reclaim the backend and exit too — no zombie processes |

None of the three "hide to tray" paths shows any notification, and none of them stops the backend.

Hotkeys are intercepted only while the **app window is focused**, so they never steal `Alt+F4` / `Ctrl+Shift+W` from other programs.

### How restart works

When the backend's main process exits, the launcher does **not** reclaim immediately. It enters a "restart watch window" (30 s by default):

1. the port is being listened on again → treat it as a successful restart, adopt the new process and keep supervising; tray icon and window stay put;
2. no process left in the job, or the watch window expires → treat it as a real exit and reclaim everything.

The signal is the **port**, not the main process handle, because dshmarket restarts by having the main process kill itself and letting a **detached helper** bring a replacement up once the port is free. That helper is still inside the launcher's Job Object (measured), so watching the main process handle used to kill the helper and its replacement too — which is exactly why "click Restart" used to leave the tray icon gone and the page spinning.


### A tray restart also swaps the app window

The tray item (and `--restart`) relaunches the backend **and** opens a fresh app window with the new token, then closes the old one. If the old window was hidden in the tray, the new one keeps that state so nothing pops up. The destroy hook is re-armed for the new window, so closing the old one is not mistaken for "the user quit".

Why: backing only the backend and letting the page merely reconnect leaves the page on stale client code, and it walks into the "the host's projections are still cold" race — `dsh-notification` folds an absent projection as turn 0, so every session with a completed turn looks like it just advanced and a burst of notifications fires. A fresh window is equivalent to the full reload dshmarket does.

If the UI still does not come back after a tray restart (say a bundle that cannot be fetched kept one plugin out of sync), press **Ctrl+R** in the window — it stays open, so you can refresh any time.

